  "issue_comments": [
    {
      "body": "Agent:\r\n\r\nThe research supports keeping the change. The strongest evidence is upstream history: this appears to fix a stale guard left behind by an earlier refactor.\r\n\r\nMerged [PR #25539 (https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/25539)](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/25539) moved the assistant seed forward from eager execution into draft_forward(), specifically to avoid its extra launch and synchronization overhead. It also removed a num_steps > 1 condition around attention initialization, explaining that initialization must now run even with one step.\r\n\r\nI inspected that PR’s merged code: it retained the separate num_steps <= 1 graph-capture guard, despite moving a real model forward into the one-step draft path. That mismatch is concrete evidence for changing the threshold. The conclusion that it was overlooked is my inference; I found no documented reason for deliberately keeping one-step frozen-KV drafting eager.\r\n\r\nOrdinary EAGLE remains different. Its separate implementation still skips capture for one step and explicitly comments that draft_forward only performs sampling in that case. This PR changes the frozen-KV worker’s guard, leaving EAGLE’s condition intact. [EAGLE source at the PR revision (https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/767fa00745f1d16f205a50cf5c47b3ff9186bf7e/python/sglang/srt/speculative/eagle_worker_v2.py#L656)](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/767fa00745f1d16f205a50cf5c47b3ff9186bf7e/python/sglang/srt/speculative/eagle_worker_v2.py#L656)\r\n\r\nThe performance rationale also matches PyTorch’s guidance: graphs reduce CPU dispatch overhead for repeated, graph-safe GPU work, especially when individual operations are short. One model forward can contain many kernel launches; it need not contain multiple recurrent steps to benefit. [PyTorch documentation (https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/notes/cuda#cuda-graphs)](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/notes/cuda#cuda-graphs)\r\n\r\nMy recommendation is to retain the implementation and add a one-step eager-versus-replay regression before merging. That would address the validation gap. Restricting the fix to XPU solely because the benchmark used XPU would be unnecessarily narrow—the architectural rationale applies to CUDA too, although CUDA still needs validation.\r\n\r\nMy earlier answers were too categorical before checking this history. The historical evidence makes the implementation substantially better justified; it does not establish correctness across every backend configuration.",
      "created_at": "2026-10-17T05:26:50Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/44854#issuecomment-6230170451",
